 "name": "Favourite colour and colour pair (Jastrow 1897 World's Fair colour census)",
 "description": "Method replication of Joseph Jastrow's colour census at the Psychological Laboratory of the World's Columbian Exposition, Chicago 1893 (Popular Science Monthly 50, 1897, pp. 361–368). Participants choose the most pleasing of 24 colours (12 normal hues and their 12 lighter shades, numbered 1–24 as on the original placard, 25 = no choice), then the most pleasing of 24 two-colour combinations (A–X, Y = no choice), and give sex and age. Values use the placard's numbers and letters, so results can be compared with Jastrow's: blue first (just over a quarter of 4,556 votes), then red; orange shades least chosen; men favoured blue, women red; favourite pairs red–violet and red–blue. Differences from the original study: (1) the original used coloured papers from the Prang Educational Company; the colours here are screen approximations of the named hues, so exact shades differ; (2) voters dropped a numbered card into a box (card shape = sex, age written on the back); here the choices are made on screen; (3) sex has an \"other / prefer not to say\" option. Public domain source.",
